+       Both halves wrong. `metricRows.ts` declares **17** rows, **12** render, and **"Ø Key passes"
+       appears on ZERO built pages** — it is one of the rows the committed sample cannot feed. I
+       carried the framing from `!128`, where `defensive_actions_per_match` genuinely renders.
+       ⭐ What DOES discriminate, found by looking: `check-metric-labels.test.mjs` asserts the
+       `metricRows.ts` ↔ `strings.ts` binding in BOTH directions in all three locales, so renaming
+       one side alone turns `npm test` red. `!125` measured that this guard covers only TEAM
+       `metrics.*` keys — which is precisely why it is load-bearing HERE.
+    2. **"The block count falls to 171."** It ROSE to 175. −2 predicted orphans, **+4 unpredicted**:
+       the CPO-ruled rename moves four columns from `key_passes_*` (decomposes onto `key_passes`,
+       NOT a player metric_id, so no block) to `passes_key_player_*` (decomposes onto a real player
+       metric, so blocks appear). I reasoned about the half of the mechanism I had seen before.
+    3. **"That's a documentation improvement — four columns can now carry a description."** Also
+       wrong, and only checked because (2) had just failed. **All four new blocks are ORPHANS** —
+       zero references, exactly like the two that disappeared. Nothing gained a description. The
+       honest statement is *two orphan blocks replaced by four*.
+  ⭐⭐ **THE RULE: A NUMBER PREDICTED FROM A PREVIOUS BATCH'S MECHANISM IS A CLAIM, NOT EVIDENCE.
+  When it misses, the miss IS the finding** — and the only reason all three were caught is that each
+  prediction was written down first and then measured, rather than restated.
+
+  ⭐ **THE FRONTEND CHANGES FOR THE FIRST TIME IN STEP 4.** `site_v2/src/lib/types.ts:53` declares
+  `passes_key` on the `TopPlayer` interface, and `top_players[]` comes from `mart_player_momentum`,
+  which renames it — so the type would otherwise declare a field the payload stops carrying. One
+  token, one file; the other four swept frontend files are byte-identical. MRs 1-3 each asserted an
+  EMPTY `site_v2/` diff, and repeating that claim here would have been the same inherited-claim
+  defect that cost `!128` two rounds.
+
+  ⭐ **THE JUNCTION AGAIN, AND THIS TIME A STEM REACHES IT DIRECTLY.**
+  `int_legs__team_from_players.sql:28` reads `sum(passes_key) as key_passes` — player name in, TEAM
+  name out; RULING 5 named key passes among its seven team metrics. Provider-excluded, verified
+  untouched. `int_legs__team_match.sql:120-121` also carries `own.passes_total` /
+  `own.passes_accurate`, TEAM leg columns sharing the player metric names.
+  ⭐ **Bare `key_passes` was SWEPT deliberately even though it never moves** — 14 printed PROTECT
+  decisions instead of 14 invisible exclusions, per step 3's rule that a reviewer can check a
+  decision list but not an exclusion kept in someone's head.
+
+  ⚠ **#96 AT ITS WIDEST: THREE of six lists, SEVEN entries.** Both 18-name PLAYER lists change for
+  the first time (`key_passes_per90`, `pass_accuracy_pct`) while `passes_per90` must not move; the
+  14-name board list moves three. The three 22-name TEAM lists are untouched — and the substring
+  hazard step 3 recorded did NOT fire: `passes_accuracy_pct` is intact, not `passes_accuracy_pct_pct`.
+  ⚠ FOUR singular range tests renamed — a surface `!127` and `!128` had none of, and one step 3
+  PROTECTED as "step-4 names". ⚠ My first check of them asserted the `*pass*_in_range` set was
+  exactly those four; there are EIGHT, the other four being TEAM tests that must not move. Both
+  halves are now pinned, which is stronger than pinning one.
+
+  ⭐ The 1604-description count is the "re-point them" ruling measured a fifth time, identical to
+  the base.
+
+  ⛔⛔ **ROUND 1 FAILED 4–1 ON A NON-COMPILING DEFECT, AND IT IS THE ALIAS HEURISTIC AGAIN.**
+  `analytics-engineer-reviewer` found three models shipping
+  `sum(round(passes_player * passes_accuracy_percent / 100)) as passes_accurate_player` — a nested
+  aggregate over a SIBLING ALIAS, against CTEs that carry only `passes_total`:
+      int_player_club_season__metrics.sql:125 · int_player_season_position__metrics.sql:106
+      int_player_season_record.sql:54
+  ⛔ **THE CAUSE IS THE MISTAKE THIS LOG ALREADY RECORDS.** The classifier decided "is this an
+  upstream read?" by asking whether the LINE ends with `as <the same token>`. That recognises
+  `sum(passes_total) as passes_player` and MISSES `sum(round(passes_total * …)) as
+  passes_accurate_player`, which is just as much an upstream read — the metric is COMPUTED FROM the
+  provider column, so the alias is a different word. `!125`'s round-2 entry says, verbatim, that for
+  bare reads I asked "does the alias happen to match the inner name" instead of "did the source
+  relation rename this column". **Same mistake, third shape, four batches apart.**
+  ⭐ **THE FIX DELETES THE HEURISTIC RATHER THAN PATCHING IT.** Every bare read now resolves its
+  scope's SOURCE: a source CTE that WRITES the token emits the new name (aliases always move) →
+  RENAME; a chain reaching a provider relation → PROTECT; a renaming relation → RENAME; **anything
+  else ABORTS.** The abort fired twice on the first run — a jinja macro with no query scope, and
+  tokens inside `{# #}` comments — and both were real gaps in my model of the files, not noise.
+  ⚠ One inversion inside the new rule had to be corrected too: a source CTE that aliases the token
+  still shows the OLD name in the base text, but that alias moves, so the read moves with it.
+  Reading the base and concluding "protect" is the same pre-vs-post error `!125` made in the
+  yml-column guard; it inverted 17 decisions before being caught. Counts: **275/151 → 267/159**.
+
+  ⛔⛔ **AND THE RESOLVER COULD NOT HAVE CAUGHT IT — THREE BLIND SPOTS, FOUND BY EXPERIMENT.** I
+  first told the CPO that `!128`'s exemption had created a false negative here. **That was wrong.**
+  Reintroducing the defect and running the tool gave **exit 0**, and the reason was not the
+  exemption at all:
+    · `check_bare` walked only CTE BODIES, so a read in a model's FINAL SELECT was never scanned —
+      which is exactly where `int_player_season_record.sql:54` lives;
+    · `window w as (…)` clauses parsed as CTEs, pushing the synthesised final-select scope past the
+      end of the file even after that scope was added;
+    · a CTE that JOINS two relations resolved to only one, reporting `int_team_season_record.sql:141`
+      broken — a FALSE POSITIVE on a file this branch never touched, the cries-wolf class a third time.
+  All three closed, each verified by watching the real defect go RED with the exact line and the
+  untouched file go GREEN. The `!128` `created` exemption was additionally made SCOPE-AWARE: a name
+  aliased in the SAME select as the read is not exempt, because SQL cannot see a sibling alias.
+  ⭐⭐ **THE RULE: A FIX THAT REMOVES A FALSE POSITIVE CAN INSTALL A FALSE NEGATIVE, AND ONLY
+  RE-PROVING THE ORIGINAL DEFECT STILL GOES RED WILL TELL YOU.** Twice in two batches.
+  ⛔ **AND THE HONEST READ ON THE OPEN CI-GATE QUESTION:** two batches of green results had concealed
+  three separate blind spots in this tool. It is not close to committable. "Harden, then commit" is
+  no longer a preference — it is what the evidence says.
+
+  ⚠ A miscount of mine, caught independently by `bi-analyst-reviewer` and `platform-reviewer`:
+  `metricRows.ts` declares **16** rows, not the 17 I wrote. I counted `field:` string occurrences,
+  which double-counts the nested `team:` override on the `clean_sheets` row. The conclusion it
+  supported is unchanged and both reviewers re-derived it: 16 − 4 rows the sample cannot feed = 12
+  rendered, and "Ø Key passes" renders nowhere.
+
+  ⛔⛔ **ROUND 2 THEN FAILED TOO — 3 PASS / 2 FAIL — ON A FOURTH SITE OF THE SAME CLASS**, found
+  INDEPENDENTLY by `analytics-engineer-reviewer` and `bi-analyst-reviewer`.
+  `mart_player_season_record.sql:168-170,187`: the `matched` CTE was correctly updated to
+  `sf.passes_key_player` / `sf.passes_accurate_player` / `sf.passes_player`, but the model's own
+  FINAL SELECT still projected the OLD bare names, which no longer exist in that chain — while its
+  own `shared.yml` entry already declared the new ones. A fourth model that could not compile.
+  ⛔ **THE CAUSE, a THIRD root under the same rule: NEITHER TOOL FOLLOWED `JOIN`.** `matched` is
+  `from sides as s inner join season_final as sf` and the renamed columns come from the JOINED side;
+  my resolution chased `sides` and answered from the wrong branch. Both the classifier and the
+  resolver reported the tree clean — twice.
+  ⭐ Fixed by resolving across the WHOLE source set: every `ref()`, `from` AND `join`, walked
+  transitively; RENAME if any terminal renames, PROTECT only if EVERY terminal is a provider
+  relation, **ABORT otherwise**. Two more aborts fired at once and were real: window clauses
+  (`w as (…)`) parsing as CTEs had been hiding the final select of `int_player_season_record.sql`
+  from resolution entirely. Counts across the three rounds: **275/151 → 267/159 → 272/154.**
+
+  ⭐⭐ **THE ENTRY WORTH KEEPING: WHEN EVERY FAILURE COMES FROM ONE KIND OF LOGIC, STOP FIXING IT
+  AND BUILD A CHECK THAT CANNOT FAIL THAT WAY.** Four missed sites across two rounds all came from
+  my own source resolution — alias heuristics, chain walking, join following. Before spending the
+  LAST round I built one that is independent of all of it: **for every model, do the columns its yml
+  declares actually appear in its FINAL SELECT projection?** That is precisely the mismatch
+  `bi-analyst-reviewer` used to find the round-2 site, and it needs no notion of CTEs, joins or
+  aliases at all.
+  ⚠ It also shows why the pre-existing yml guard was too weak: it asked whether the new name appears
+  ANYWHERE in the file, and `passes_key_player` did — inside a CTE. The new check reads only the
+  final projection.
+  ⭐ Result: **10 models, exactly one mismatch — the site the reviewers had found, and no fifth.**
+  That is what justified spending round 3 rather than guessing.
+  ⭐⭐ **AND IT WAS THEN RUN AS AN AUDIT OF ALREADY-MERGED WORK — `!125`, `!127`, `!128`: 9 models,
+  ZERO mismatches.** The three merged batches are sound. Worth doing whenever a defect class is
+  discovered late: ask whether it also shipped.
+
+  ⛔ **WHAT THIS SETTLES ABOUT THE OPEN CI-GATE QUESTION.** Across MR 4 the column-reference resolver
+  was found to have FOUR blind spots — CTE-bodies-only scanning, window clauses parsed as CTEs,
+  single-branch join resolution, and a silent skip when a scope's relation could not be resolved,
+  which is the "reported, never skipped" property I had claimed it has. Two batches of green results
+  had concealed all four. **It is not committable, and "harden, then commit" is now what the
+  evidence says rather than a preference.** The check that actually caught things — yml vs final
+  projection — is simpler than the resolver and is the better candidate if a gate is ever wanted.
+
